A parse thread's work on a file in a scope of the host's

What a parse thread makes of a file (its text, its tree, its symbols) all outlives the thread, and
none of it is written after the file is published. A host can use that: garnet's arena keeps what
is allocated inside it without tracing it and publishes it when it ends, so the publish of a batch
no longer walks every node of every file (19% of the parse threads' time). `startThreads` takes the
host's scope function, and `PreparingHost` reads, parses and binds a file inside it
(`ParallelParse.scope`; TSC_TS_ARENA=0 for none under any host). The file keeps its own copy of the
loader's `ExternalModuleIndicatorOptions`, made with it: the one object a file referred to that the
loader had made before (garnet's TSAOT_ARENA_CHECK found it).

The compiler project under garnet, nine alternating runs of one build with and without: the parse
threads' instructions 2.903 -> 2.562 G (-11.7%), every thread's 16.207 -> 15.825 G (-2.35%), the
other files parsed at 38.0 ms where it was 40.7 (median). What the compiler reports is unchanged.

